<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manusmriti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 200 BCE–<a href="/years/200">200 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Manusmṛti, Mānava-Dharmaśāstra, Manava Dharmashastra, Laws of Manu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Manusmriti, also known as the Manava <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmashastra">Dharmashastra</a> or the Laws of Manu, is a major legal text among the Dharmashastras of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> work in verse that most scholars date to between 200 BCE and 200 CE. It presents itself as a discourse by Manu and Bhrigu on duties, laws, conduct, gender norms and virtues, and is best known for its different rules for the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a>. Its influence reached Hindu kingdoms in what are now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and it was used to construct the Hindu law code of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s enclaves. In modern times it is controversial for its treatment of women and lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> burned copies in 1927 to protest caste discrimination.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Manusmriti, also called the Manava Dharmashastra, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> text in verse, usually placed between 200 BCE and 200 CE. The title Manusmriti is a relatively modern term, probably coined because the text is in verse; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> never use it and give the title Manava Dharmashastra in the colophons of their chapters.</p><p>Early philologists dated the work far too early, William Jones to about 1250 BCE and Karl Wilhelm Friedrich Schlegel to about 1000 BCE, though its language must be later than the late Vedic texts. It presents itself as a dialogue in which Manu teaches and his student Bhrigu speaks most of the verses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The traditional twelve chapters do not go back to the original text. The work moves from the creation of the world to the sources of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>, then to the dharma of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a>, and ends with the law of karma, rebirth and final liberation. It names as sources of law the whole Veda, then the tradition and conduct of those who know it, the customs of holy men and self-satisfaction.</p><p>Its largest portions are on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, with 1,034 verses, and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriyas</a>, with 971; rules for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishya">Vaishyas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudras</a> are brief. A long section on kings covers officials, taxation, war and eighteen grounds for litigation, and the laws of war say that civilians and those who have surrendered must not be harmed. The text imposes many restrictions on women, who it says should never be independent, and it forbids widows to remarry, although other verses allow remarriage in cases of abandonment or abuse.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Over fifty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> are known, and they differ from one another. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> manuscript with the commentary of Kulluka Bhatta was long presumed authentic, and it was translated by Jones, Burnell, Bühler and Doniger.</p><p>Some scholars doubt that it was ever administered as law in ancient or medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and David Buxbaum called it in great part an ideal picture of what a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> held ought to be law. It was nevertheless honoured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, Siam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and Java-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, where it was copied and built into local codes, and British officials used it to construct a law for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s enclaves.</p><p>Critics condemn its rules on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudras</a>, who it says cannot be released from servitude, and on women. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> attended a conference at which copies were burned in 1927 to protest against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> discrimination.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manusmriti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chenla's kings took the Sanskrit name of a patron deity or avatar with the suffix -varman, &quot;protected by&quot;, following the code of the Kshatriya caste in the <b>Laws of Manu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The law books, of which the <b>Manusmriti</b> is the most famous, set out the duties of the classes and stages of life.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>…1789; the Puranas, encyclopedic collections of myth and history; the treatises on law and statecraft, such as the <b>Laws of Manu</b> and the Arthashastra; the Kamasutra; the theory of drama and aesthetic emotion of the Natyashastra; and the animal fables of the Panchatantra, which travelled through Middle Persian…</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The text was influenced by Hindu texts such as the sections on kings, governance and legal procedures in the <b>Manusmriti</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/arthashastra">Arthashastra · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Manusmriti</b> gave the Chandalas the carrying away of corpses and the execution of condemned criminals.</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote>The Brahmanical law books of this age, the Dharmashastras such as the <b>Manusmriti</b>, ranked people in four varnas fixed by birth: Brahmins, Kshatriyas, Vaishyas and Shudras, with groups such as the Chandalas placed outside them.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classical-india">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</a></li></ul></section>
